1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:06,320
You swipe your credit card for a ten million dong dinner banquet and celebrate receiving one hundred thousand dong in instant cashback rewards.

2
00:00:06,320 --> 00:00:07,850
But who actually funded that reward?

3
00:00:08,850 --> 00:00:12,620
At the exact second the terminal beeps, zero cash leaves your possession.

4
00:00:12,620 --> 00:00:19,170
Your issuing bank merely freezes ten million of your credit line as an authorization hold, advancing the real money days later.

5
00:00:19,770 --> 00:00:26,360
Two business days later, the restaurant receives only nine million seven hundred and eighty thousand dong.

6
00:00:26,360 --> 00:00:32,330
Exactly two hundred and twenty thousand dong was deducted up front as a merchant processing fee.

7
00:00:33,060 --> 00:00:46,050
The largest portion of that processing fee — approximately one hundred and fifty thousand dong — travels straight to your card issuing bank as an interchange settlement fee to compensate for credit float.

8
00:00:46,730 --> 00:00:52,180
That interchange fee is the primary commercial engine funding your reward programs.

9
00:00:52,180 --> 00:00:59,210
The issuing bank captures a fee from the merchant and returns a smaller slice to you as marketing cashback.

10
00:01:00,010 --> 00:01:09,490
And because restaurants incorporate payment processing overhead into standard retail prices, your shiny cashback reward was simply your own dining money completing an economic circle.
